WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.199 --> 00:00:04.180
Dear Fellow Scholars, this is Two Minute Papers
with Károly Zsolnai-Fehér.

00:00:04.180 --> 00:00:09.240
In a previous episode, we talked about a class
of learning algorithms that were endowed with

00:00:09.240 --> 00:00:10.370
curiosity.

00:00:10.370 --> 00:00:16.090
This new work also showcases a curious AI
that aims to solve Montezuma's revenge which

00:00:16.090 --> 00:00:20.030
is a notoriously difficult platform game for
an AI to finish.

00:00:20.030 --> 00:00:25.070
The main part of the difficulty arises from
the fact that the AI needs to be able to plan

00:00:25.070 --> 00:00:31.190
for longer time periods, and interestingly,
it also needs to learn that short-term rewards

00:00:31.190 --> 00:00:33.960
don't necessarily mean long-term success.

00:00:33.960 --> 00:00:36.470
Let's have a look at an example - quoting
the authors:

00:00:36.470 --> 00:00:40.980
"There are four keys and six doors spread
throughout the level.

00:00:40.980 --> 00:00:46.220
Any of the four keys can open any of the six
doors, but are consumed in the process.

00:00:46.220 --> 00:00:51.760
To open the final two doors the agent must
therefore forego opening two of the doors

00:00:51.760 --> 00:00:56.260
that are easier to find and that would immediately
reward it for opening them."

00:00:56.260 --> 00:01:00.559
So what this means is that we have a tricky
situation, because the agent would have to

00:01:00.559 --> 00:01:06.369
disregard the fact that it is getting a nice
score from opening the doors, and understand

00:01:06.369 --> 00:01:09.060
that these keys can be saved for later.

00:01:09.060 --> 00:01:15.070
This is very hard for an AI to resist, and,
again, curiosity comes to the rescue.

00:01:15.070 --> 00:01:21.350
Curiosity, at least, this particular definition
of it works in a way that the harder to guess

00:01:21.350 --> 00:01:26.509
for the AI what will happen, the more excited
it gets to perform an action.

00:01:26.509 --> 00:01:31.479
This drives the agent to finish the game and
explore as much as possible because it is

00:01:31.479 --> 00:01:34.270
curious to see what the next level holds.

00:01:34.270 --> 00:01:39.520
You see in the animation here that the big
reward spikes show that the AI has found something

00:01:39.520 --> 00:01:45.570
new and meaningful, like losing a life, or
narrowly avoiding an adversary.

00:01:45.570 --> 00:01:51.920
As you also see, climbing a ladder is a predictable,
boring mechanic that the AI is not very excited

00:01:51.920 --> 00:01:52.920
about.

00:01:52.920 --> 00:01:58.869
Later, it becomes able to predict the results
even better the second and third time around,

00:01:58.869 --> 00:02:02.430
therefore it gets even less excited about
ladders.

00:02:02.430 --> 00:02:08.569
This other animation shows how this curious
agent explores adjacent rooms over time.

00:02:08.569 --> 00:02:14.010
This work also introduces a technique, which
the authors call random network distillation.

00:02:14.010 --> 00:02:19.280
This means that we start out from a completely
randomly initialized, untrained neural network,

00:02:19.280 --> 00:02:23.379
and over time, slowly distill it into a trained
one.

00:02:23.379 --> 00:02:29.030
This distillation also makes our neural network
immune to the noisy TV problem from our previous

00:02:29.030 --> 00:02:35.200
episode, where our curious, unassuming agent
would get stuck in front of a TV that continually

00:02:35.200 --> 00:02:37.290
plays new content.

00:02:37.290 --> 00:02:41.870
It also takes into consideration the score
reported by the game, and has an internal

00:02:41.870 --> 00:02:44.209
motivation to explore as well.

00:02:44.209 --> 00:02:49.299
And hold on to your papers, because it can
not only perform well in the game, but this

00:02:49.299 --> 00:02:53.700
AI is able to perform better than the average
human.

00:02:53.700 --> 00:02:58.690
And again, remember that no ground truth knowledge
is required, it was never demonstrated to

00:02:58.690 --> 00:03:01.660
the AI how one should play this game.

00:03:01.660 --> 00:03:06.989
Very impressive results indeed, and you see,
the pace of progress in machine learning research

00:03:06.989 --> 00:03:09.019
is nothing short of incredible.

00:03:09.019 --> 00:03:12.359
Make sure to have a look at the paper in the
video description for more details.

00:03:12.359 --> 00:03:17.739
We'd also like to send a big thank you to
Insilico Medicine for supporting this video.

00:03:17.739 --> 00:03:23.340
They use AI for research on preventing aging,
believe it or not, and are doing absolutely

00:03:23.340 --> 00:03:24.640
amazing work.

00:03:24.640 --> 00:03:27.140
Make sure to check them out in the video description.

00:03:27.140 --> 00:03:29.689
Thanks for watching and for your generous
support, and I'll see you next time!

